course: dictionary
lesson: 13054
title: Fair and unfair: favoritism
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Favoritism means treating one person better than others because you like them more, not because they deserve it. Look at the root word: a favorite is the one you like best. Favoritism is what happens when that liking changes how someone is treated.
explain: «Mrs Patel always lets Ruby answer first, even when Sam has his hand up.» Sam is waiting with his hand up, but he is still ignored. Try your meaning in the sentence: "Mrs Patel treats Ruby better because she likes her more." It still makes sense, so favoritism fits.
explain: Having a favorite is not wrong, but acting as if one person matters more than everyone else is unfair. In the Bible, Jacob gave his son Joseph a special coat, and his other sons became jealous. That is favoritism causing trouble between brothers.
fact: Favoritism means unfairly treating one person or group better than others, because you like them more.
fact: The root word is favorite. Favoritism is when a favorite gets special treatment they have not earned.
fact: In British English the spelling is «favouritism», with a u.
fact: You can «show favoritism», and someone who says you did it has «accused» you «of favoritism».
fact: In jobs and government, favoritism is unfair and can be corruption, when people in power help friends instead of the best person for the job.
exercise: «Mrs Patel always lets Ruby answer first, even when Sam has his hand up.» What is this an example of? | Good listening | Being very kind to everyone | *Favoritism | A fair turn for each child
exercise: «The coach picked his nephew for the team, even though other players were faster.» What is the unfair part? | The coach picked the fastest player | The nephew was too young to play | *The coach chose a player because he liked him, not because he deserved it | The coach forgot to write the team list
exercise: What does favoritism mean? | Giving everyone exactly the same chance | *Treating one person better than others because you like them more | Winning a race fairly | Being a good friend to a new pupil
test: «The judge was accused of favoritism when he gave the job to his own friend, not the best applicant.» What does accused of favoritism mean here? | *Someone said he treated his friend better without a fair reason | Someone thanked him for a fair decision | He was given a medal for good work | He was asked to make a speech
test: Which word is the root of favoritism? | friend | fair | *favorite | forgive
exam: Which sentence shows favoritism? | «The referee showed the same rules to both teams.» | «Every child in the class got the same chance to speak.» | *«The manager gave the best shift to her own daughter, though others had asked first.»
